\title{A Multi-Kernel Support Law for Sidon Sets: Exact Difference-Set Cardinality and a Sharp Characterization}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{2026-07-04}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
A finite set of integers is a \emph{Sidon set} (or $B_2$ set) if all of its pairwise sums are distinct. We study the additive structure of such sets through a pair of convolution kernels---the sum kernel and the difference kernel---and establish an exact support law for the difference side. First, for a nonempty Sidon set $s$ of cardinality $k$, the difference set attains the maximal possible size, $|s-s| = k^2 - k + 1$, equivalently $|s-s| + k = k^2 + 1$. Second, this extremal identity \emph{characterizes} the Sidon property: a nonempty finite set of integers is Sidon if and only if $|s-s| = k^2 - k + 1$. Both statements factor through a single structural equivalence---a set is Sidon precisely when the ordered-difference map is injective on the off-diagonal. Combined with the classical sum-side identity $2\,|s+s| = k(k+1)$, we obtain a linear conservation law relating the two kernels, $2\,|s+s| = |s-s| + 2k - 1$. We discuss an $O(k^2)$ Sidon-detection algorithm, applications to autocorrelation-flat signal design and difference families, and a stability program measuring the deficit $k^2 - k + 1 - |s-s|$ as a quantitative distance to Sidon.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}

Sidon sets, introduced by Simon Sidon in the 1930s in connection with lacunary Fourier series, are among the most fundamental objects in additive combinatorics. A finite set $s \subseteq \Z$ is a \emph{Sidon set} if the equation $a + b = c + d$ with $a,b,c,d \in s$ forces $\{a,b\} = \{c,d\}$; equivalently, all differences of distinct elements are pairwise distinct. Such sets are extremal for a variety of counting problems: they have the fewest repeated sums, the smallest additive energy, and---as we make precise here---the largest possible difference set.

Modern treatments approach the additive structure of a set $s$ through its \emph{representation kernels}. The \emph{sum kernel} is the autoconvolution
\[
  r^{+}_s(x) = (\ind_s * \ind_s)(x) = \#\{(a,b) \in s \times s : a + b = x\},
\]
and the \emph{difference kernel} is the autocorrelation
\[
  r^{-}_s(x) = (\ind_s \star \ind_s)(x) = \#\{(a,b) \in s \times s : a - b = x\}.
\]
The supports of these two kernels are the sumset $s + s$ and the difference set $s - s$. Together the pair $(r^{+}_s, r^{-}_s)$ constitutes a \emph{multi-kernel} description of $s$: the sum kernel governs sumset size and additive energy, while the difference kernel---the focus of this paper---governs the geometry of pairwise gaps.

The classical theory pins down the sum side. For a Sidon set of size $k$, the $\binom{k}{2}$ sums of distinct pairs and the $k$ diagonal sums $2a$ are all distinct, giving
\begin{equation}\label{eq:sum}
  |s + s| = \binom{k}{2} + k = \frac{k(k+1)}{2}, \qquad \text{i.e.} \qquad 2\,|s+s| = k(k+1).
\end{equation}
This paper supplies the missing \emph{exact} difference-side law and shows it is sharp enough to \emph{characterize} the Sidon property.

\subsection*{Contributions}
\begin{enumerate}
  \item Exact difference-set cardinality (Theorem~\ref{thm:card}): for a nonempty Sidon set of size $k$, $|s-s| = k^2 - k + 1$.
  \item Sharp characterization (Theorem~\ref{thm:char}): a nonempty finite set is Sidon iff $|s-s| = k^2 - k + 1$.
  \item Conservation law (Corollary~\ref{cor:cons}): $2\,|s+s| = |s-s| + 2k - 1$ for every nonempty Sidon set.
  \item An $O(k^2)$ detection algorithm and a stability/deficit framework.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Definitions}

Throughout, $s$ denotes a finite subset of $\Z$ and $k = |s|$.

\begin{definition}[Sidon set]
A finite set $s \subseteq \Z$ is a \emph{Sidon set} if for all $a,b,c,d \in s$,
\[
  a + b = c + d \implies a = c \ \text{or}\ a = d.
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Difference set and sumset]
$s - s = \{a - b : a,b \in s\}$ and $s + s = \{a + b : a,b \in s\}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Off-diagonal and difference map]
The \emph{off-diagonal} of $s$ is $s^{\ne} = \{(a,b) \in s \times s : a \ne b\}$, of cardinality $k^2 - k$. The \emph{difference map} is $\delta:\Z\times\Z\to\Z$, $\delta(a,b) = a - b$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[No zeros off the diagonal]\label{lem:nozero}
For every $(a,b) \in s^{\ne}$, $\delta(a,b) \ne 0$; hence $0 \notin \delta(s^{\ne})$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
$\delta(a,b) = a - b = 0$ iff $a = b$, excluded on the off-diagonal.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Decomposition of the difference set]\label{lem:decomp}
For any nonempty $s$, $\ s - s = \{0\} \cup \delta(s^{\ne})$, and the union is disjoint. Consequently
\begin{equation}\label{eq:book}
  |s - s| = |\delta(s^{\ne})| + 1.
\end{equation}
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
If $x = a - b \in s-s$ with $a=b$ then $x = 0$; otherwise $(a,b)\in s^{\ne}$ and $x \in \delta(s^{\ne})$. Conversely $0 = c-c$ for any $c \in s$ (nonemptiness), and each element of $\delta(s^{\ne})$ is a difference. Disjointness is Lemma~\ref{lem:nozero}; \eqref{eq:book} follows by counting.
\end{proof}

\section{The structural equivalence}

\begin{theorem}[Sidon $\Leftrightarrow$ injective difference map]\label{thm:equiv}
A finite set $s \subseteq \Z$ is Sidon if and only if $\delta$ is injective on the off-diagonal $s^{\ne}$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$(\Rightarrow)$ Let $s$ be Sidon and $(a,b),(c,d) \in s^{\ne}$ with $a - b = c - d$. Then $a + d = c + b$, and the Sidon condition gives $a = c$ or $a = b$. The latter is impossible off-diagonal, so $a = c$, whence $b = d$.

$(\Leftarrow)$ Let $\delta$ be injective on $s^{\ne}$ and $a+b = c+d$. If $a = d$ we are done; assume $a \ne d$. If $c = b$ then $a + b = b + d$ gives $a = d$, a contradiction, so $c \ne b$. Then $(a,d),(c,b) \in s^{\ne}$ and $a - d = c - b$, i.e. $\delta(a,d) = \delta(c,b)$; injectivity gives $(a,d) = (c,b)$, so $a = c$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
Injectivity must be asserted on the off-diagonal: the diagonal always collapses to $0$ under $\delta$, so $\delta$ is never injective on $s\times s$ for $k\ge 2$. Nonemptiness is needed in the cardinality statements because \eqref{eq:book} inserts the value $0$.
\end{remark}

\section{Main results}

\begin{theorem}[Maximal difference-set cardinality]\label{thm:card}
Let $s \subseteq \Z$ be a nonempty Sidon set with $k = |s|$. Then
\[
  |s - s| + k = k^2 + 1, \qquad \text{equivalently} \qquad |s - s| = k^2 - k + 1.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:equiv}, $\delta$ is injective on $s^{\ne}$, so $|\delta(s^{\ne})| = |s^{\ne}| = k^2 - k$. Substituting into \eqref{eq:book}, $|s-s| = (k^2-k) + 1$. Adding $k$ gives $|s-s| + k = k^2 + 1$.
\end{proof}

For any $k$-element set the difference set contains $0$ and at most $k^2 - k$ nonzero values, so $|s-s| \le k^2 - k + 1$ unconditionally; Theorem~\ref{thm:card} says Sidon sets meet this bound exactly.

\begin{theorem}[Characterization by extremal difference set]\label{thm:char}
Let $s \subseteq \Z$ be nonempty with $k = |s|$. Then $s$ is Sidon if and only if
\[
  |s - s| = k^2 - k + 1, \qquad \text{i.e.} \qquad |s-s| + k = k^2 + 1.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Forward direction is Theorem~\ref{thm:card}. Conversely, if $|s-s| = k^2 - k + 1$ then by \eqref{eq:book} $|\delta(s^{\ne})| = k^2 - k = |s^{\ne}|$. A map whose image matches the (finite) domain cardinality is injective, so $\delta$ is injective on $s^{\ne}$ and Theorem~\ref{thm:equiv} gives that $s$ is Sidon.
\end{proof}

Define the \emph{deficit} $D(s) = (k^2 - k + 1) - |s-s| \ge 0$. Then $D(s) = 0$ iff $s$ is Sidon, and $D(s)$ measures the collapse of the difference map's image, i.e. the number of coincidences $a - b = c - d$ among distinct pairs.

\begin{corollary}[Sum--difference conservation law]\label{cor:cons}
For every nonempty Sidon set $s$ of size $k$,
\[
  2\,|s + s| = |s - s| + 2k - 1.
\]
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
By \eqref{eq:sum}, $2\,|s+s| = k(k+1) = k^2 + k$. By Theorem~\ref{thm:card}, $|s-s| + 2k - 1 = (k^2 - k + 1) + 2k - 1 = k^2 + k$.
\end{proof}

\subsection*{Worked example}
Let $s = \{1,2,4,8\}$, so $k = 4$; it is Sidon. Then $s - s = \{0,\pm1,\pm2,\pm3,\pm4,\pm6,\pm7\}$, so $|s-s| = 13 = 4^2 - 4 + 1$, confirming Theorem~\ref{thm:card}. Also $s+s = \{2,3,4,5,6,8,9,10,12,16\}$ with $|s+s| = 10 = \tfrac{4\cdot5}{2}$, and the conservation law reads $2\cdot 10 = 13 + 2\cdot 4 - 1 = 20$. The consecutive set $\{1,2,3,4\}$ has $|s-s| = 7$, deficit $D = 6 \ne 0$, so Theorem~\ref{thm:char} certifies it is not Sidon.

\section{Algorithms}

The characterization converts Sidon detection---a priori a search over $O(k^4)$ quadruples---into an $O(k^2)$ cardinality comparison.

\medskip
\noindent\textbf{Algorithm A (Difference-set Sidon test).}
\begin{enumerate}
  \item Compute all $k^2$ ordered differences $a - b$.
  \item Insert them into a hash set $D$; let $m = |D|$.
  \item Return \textsc{Sidon} iff $m = k^2 - k + 1$.
\end{enumerate}
Correctness is Theorem~\ref{thm:char}; cost is $O(k^2)$ time and space. An early-terminating variant reports failure on the first nonzero-difference collision.

\medskip
\noindent\textbf{Algorithm B (Deficit and collision profile).} Compute $D(s) = (k^2 - k + 1) - |s-s|$ and the fiber sizes $r^-_s(x)$ for $x \in s-s$. The deficit measures distance to Sidon; the fiber-size histogram is the difference kernel itself.

\section{Applications}

\emph{Autocorrelation-flat signal design.} The difference kernel $r^{-}_s$ is the aperiodic autocorrelation of the indicator sequence of $s$; Sidon sets are exactly those whose off-zero autocorrelation is $\le 1$, the design goal for radar/sonar and frequency-hopping patterns. Theorem~\ref{thm:char} gives a single-count certificate.

\emph{Difference families.} The identity $|s-s| = k^2 - k + 1$ marks the top of the hierarchy of difference-set spread over the integers, and the characterization identifies the extremal configurations.

\emph{Additive energy.} $E(s) = \sum_x r^{-}_s(x)^2 = 2k^2 - k$ for a Sidon set, the minimum over $k$-element sets; the deficit $D(s)$ and the excess energy are two linear readouts of the same fiber-size distribution.

\section{Discussion and future work}

\emph{Higher-order support laws for $B_h$ sets.} We conjecture every $B_h$ set has an $h$-fold difference set of cardinality an explicit degree-$h$ polynomial in $k$, with the $h$-fold sum and difference kernels obeying a single linear conservation identity whose corrections are the degenerate configurations counted by inclusion--exclusion; the $h=2$ stratification argument lifts to all $h$.

\emph{The conservation law modulo $n$ and perfect difference sets.} Modulo $n$ the difference kernel can wrap around; we conjecture the conservation law deforms into a corrected identity whose defect counts wrap-around collisions and vanishes exactly for translates of Singer perfect difference sets, turning the counting law into an energy identity.

\emph{Stability.} Since $|s-s| \le k^2 - k + 1$ with equality iff Sidon, the deficit $D(s)$ is a distance-to-Sidon; we conjecture it tightly controls the collision count, so a small-deficit set is close to Sidon and repairable by deleting $O(D(s))$ elements.

\section{Conclusion}

The difference kernel of a Sidon set is maximally spread: its support has exactly $k^2 - k + 1$ elements, and this extremal identity \emph{characterizes} the Sidon property. Paired with the classical sum-side identity it yields a linear conservation law. All of this flows from one structural equivalence---Sidon means the difference map is injective off the diagonal---turning a quadruple-quantified condition into a quadratic-time count, and setting the stage for higher-order, modular, and quantitative refinements.

